<!-- Application-level retints of names the framework's own control dictionaries read. Nothing here is an Astra
     resource key: every row exists because 26.10.9 paints something with a name of its own, that paint is wrong for
     a Fluent surface, and the markup that reads it cannot be replaced. A control template that owns the surface can
     be re-templated and reads Astra rows; a template that cannot be touched can only be reached through its name.
     That is the same mechanism the window-chrome and flyout-presenter aliases use (audits/window-shell.md,
     flyout-presenter.md), and the reason this dictionary is deliberately out of the key-consumption gate in
     tests/FluentJalium.Tests/Resources/AstraResourceKeyTests.cs: the reader lives in the framework, so there is no
     setter of ours that could cite it, and a gate that demanded one would only be satisfiable by a lie.
     What each row must keep doing, per row, is pinned by an Assert.Same fact in the control batch that found it. -->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.jalium.ui/2024" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!-- Found by the DataGrid batch. The runtime's DataGridRow template fills a selected row with this name, and its
       value on 26.10.9 is a brand-emerald LinearGradientBrush (#1D733C..#2B804A as measured,
       s1i-datagrid-tokens-raw.txt §J) - the exact colour this library is not allowed to show. The row cannot be
       re-templated to hold a different fill: installing a DataGridRow style strands the cell's content visual, so
       the cell renders no text at all (the reading is recorded in Styles/DataGrid.jalxaml). Aliasing rather than
       redefining is what makes this follow the application accent - both names resolve to the same brush instance,
       which is the instance FluentThemeManager.ApplyAccent and OverrideBrush move.
       Scope, measured over the framework's own dictionaries: 58 reads in twelve files (RangeControls 26, Dialogs 8,
       PropertyGrid 6, Calendar 5, ToggleControls 4, Containers 2, Navigation 2, TreeView 2, DataGrid 1,
       TreeDataGrid 1, DockLayout 1, MenusToolbars 1). Every one of them paints emerald today, so all of them change
       together. Controls this library restyles outright are unaffected - their own templates never ask for this
       name - which is what AstraDataGridTests.The_selected_row_fills_through_the_frameworks_own_name pins for the
       grid, and what the palette drift gate covers for the rest. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="AccentBrush" ResourceKey="AccentFillColorDefaultBrush" />

  <!-- Found by the A2 alias-layer batch. The runtime paints a focused control's outer border with this name, and on
       26.10.9 the value it falls back to is brand green (ThemeColors.ControlBorderFocused is an expression-bodied
       property returning Color.FromRgb(32,114,69); #FF1E793F measured off the grid host's PART_OuterBorder under the
       IsKeyboardFocused cell, s1i-datagrid-tokens-raw.txt §J) - the colour this library is not allowed to show.
       The lever was measured on the authority rather than inherited from the reference tree: the six text families
       (TextBox, PasswordBox, NumberBox, AutoCompleteBox, DatePicker, TimePicker) resolve the name per call with the
       static only as the ?? fallback, which is what
       tests/FluentJalium.Tests/AstraFrameworkNameResolutionTests.cs pins with an installed probe brush.
       Solid, not upstream's gradient, deliberately: WinUI's twin for the same construct is
       TextControlElevationBorderFocusedBrush, a LinearGradientBrush (TextBox_themeresources.xaml:57/101/164 at
       19e3bdc, stops SystemAccentColorLight2 / Dark1 over ControlStrokeColorDefault), and a gradient parked behind an
       alias row freezes on the Light stops because FluentThemeManager.RefreshPalette re-tints SolidColorBrush
       instances in place. ThemeResources/TextBox.jalxaml:21-33 already recorded that reasoning and made the same
       substitution for our own row (TextControlBorderBrushFocused -> AccentFillColorDefaultBrush); this line extends
       that one decision to the name the framework reads, so the elevation ring is a documented deviation in exactly
       one place rather than two. Controls this library restyles are unaffected - their templates ask for Astra rows. -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="ControlBorderFocused" ResourceKey="AccentFillColorDefaultBrush" />
</ResourceDictionary>
